/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_STM32
 *
 * STM32 support should be separated by family/core/FPU mode.
 *
 * Supported target presets:
 *
 * @code
 * stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft
 * stm32f103_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard
 * stm32h743_armgcc_cortex_m7f_hard
 * @endcode
 *
 * These presets map to reusable core/ABI profiles:
 *
 * | Preset | CPU | Float ABI | FPU |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft` | Cortex-M0+ | soft | none |
 * | `stm32f103_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft` | Cortex-M3 | soft | none |
 * | `stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard` | Cortex-M4 | hard | `fpv4-sp-d16` |
 * | `stm32h743_armgcc_cortex_m7f_hard` | Cortex-M7 | hard | `fpv5-d16` |
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildSTM32Rule STM32 Rule
 *
 * Do not assume all STM32 targets are compatible.
 *
 * Separate builds when any of these change:
 *
 * - Cortex core,
 * - FPU mode,
 * - compiler/toolchain,
 * - ABI,
 * - feature macros.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildSTM32Scope Library Scope
 *
 * The STM32 presets compile Foundation as a static library. They intentionally
 * do not select a concrete STM32 device macro, CMSIS package, HAL, startup file,
 * interrupt vector table, memory map, linker script, or programmer. Those belong
 * to the consuming firmware and may further narrow compatibility.
 *
 * `stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft` is the first exact STM32 device
 * profile. Its acceptance fixture in `tests/Stm32G0B1Consumer` supplies a
 * minimal vector table, startup, and memory map for the STM32G0B1CBT6 only.
 * It deliberately avoids HAL, CMSIS, clocks, GPIO, and serial output. Runtime
 * results are exposed through SWD-readable symbols, so the same firmware works
 * on a custom board without assuming its LED or UART routing.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildSTM32Commands Build and Export
 *
 * Example for STM32F407:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * arm-none-eabi-g++ --version
 * ./scripts/configure.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard --fresh
 * ./scripts/build.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard \
 *     --config Debug --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard \
 *     --config Release --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/export.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard --fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * If the compiler cannot find `stdint.h`, provide a compatible Newlib/runtime
 * and `FOUNDATION_ARM_SYSROOT`. A compiler executable without target headers is
 * not a complete cross-compilation installation.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildSTM32Validation Validation
 *
 * Use `arm-none-eabi-objdump -f` to inspect the exported Release archive and
 * verify the effective flags in `build/<preset>/compile_commands.json`. Then link
 * the package into the real firmware using the same CPU/FPU/ABI flags. Hardware
 * or emulator execution is the behavioral test; host CTest cannot execute
 * Cortex-M objects. See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation for the full
 * checklist.
 *
 * For the exact STM32G0B1CBT6 profile, build, link, and inspect the repository
 * firmware consumer with:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test-stm32.sh \
 *     stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft \
 *     --fresh --parallel 4
 * @endcode
 *
 * PowerShell exposes the same workflow:
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/test-stm32.ps1 `
 *     stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft `
 *     -Fresh -Parallel 4
 * @endcode
 *
 * This command proves the package, Cortex-M0+/soft-float identity, vector and
 * memory placement, final link, absence of undefined symbols, and the presence
 * of out-of-line Foundation Time symbols. It does not claim target execution.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildSTM32G0Hardware STM32G0B1 Hardware Acceptance
 *
 * The firmware stops in `FoundationValidationHalt()` after its checks. Load the
 * generated `FoundationStm32G0B1Consumer.elf` with STM32CubeIDE or an ST-LINK
 * GDB server, set a breakpoint on that function, reset, and run. Inspect these
 * global symbols:
 *
 * | Symbol | Passing value |
 * | ------ | ------------- |
 * | `FoundationValidationState` | `0x50415353` (`PASS`) |
 * | `FoundationValidationPassed` | `14` |
 * | `FoundationValidationTotal` | `14` |
 *
 * A command-line GDB session connected to an already-running ST-LINK GDB
 * server can use:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * arm-none-eabi-gdb FoundationStm32G0B1Consumer.elf
 * (gdb) target extended-remote localhost:61234
 * (gdb) monitor reset halt
 * (gdb) load
 * (gdb) break FoundationValidationHalt
 * (gdb) continue
 * (gdb) x/wx &FoundationValidationState
 * (gdb) x/wd &FoundationValidationPassed
 * (gdb) x/wd &FoundationValidationTotal
 * @endcode
 *
 * The GDB-server port is environment-specific; use the value reported by
 * STM32CubeIDE or STM32CubeProgrammer. Record the tool versions, exact MCU,
 * flash verification, three observed values, and the commit SHA in
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation before changing VAL-008 to `DONE`.
 */
